---
// Custom Tolo.Cloud case study. Replaces the Notion render with live
// interactives. Notion still drives metadata (title, tags, cover, order)
// for the homepage list; only the body content is custom.

import CSLayout from "../../components/case-study/CSLayout.astro";
import CSHero from "../../components/case-study/CSHero.astro";
import CSSection from "../../components/case-study/CSSection.astro";
import CSPullquote from "../../components/case-study/CSPullquote.astro";
import CSStatGrid from "../../components/case-study/CSStatGrid.astro";
import CSLiveDemo from "../../components/case-study/CSLiveDemo.astro";
import BucketCardShowcase from "../../components/case-study/showcase/BucketCardShowcase.astro";
import CardEvolutionScrubber from "../../components/case-study/showcase/CardEvolutionScrubber.astro";
import PromoteButtonDemo from "../../components/case-study/showcase/PromoteButtonDemo.astro";
import VerbsHub from "../../components/case-study/showcase/VerbsHub.astro";
import EngineerPairCard from "../../components/case-study/showcase/EngineerPairCard.astro";

export const prerender = true;
---

<CSLayout
  title="Tolo.Cloud — Jon Tollefson"
  description="The catalog became the workbench. A product-design story about an audio archive that learned to play, crop, split, promote, and mix."
  ogImage="/case-studies/the-console/cover.jpg"
>
  <CSHero
    eyebrow="Case study 02 · May 2026 — present"
    title="Tolo.Cloud"
    subtitle="From a portfolio that couldn't play a file to a catalog that crops, splits, promotes, and mixes."
    cover="/case-studies/the-console/cover.jpg"
    meta={[
      { label: "Role", value: "Designer, builder, sole engineer" },
      { label: "Timeline", value: "May 2026 — present" },
      { label: "Stack", value: "Astro · FastAPI · Python · Pedalboard · Claude" },
      { label: "Status", value: "Shipped · Engineer live 2026-05-22" },
    ]}
  />

  {/* ─────── 01 · SETUP ─────── */}
  <CSSection num="01" title="The pitch" deck="I'm a working musician with eight years of recorded ideas scattered across iCloud, an external SSD, a voice memo container, Ableton bounces on three drives, and a Splice library. I built the music catalog I'd always wanted." noTopRule>
    <div class="prose-block">
      <p>The pitch is not the catalog. The pitch is what the row inside the catalog became. The same audio that used to live as a filename on a hard drive can now: play instantly without leaving the row, be cropped to a loop with a draggable frame, be split into stems by a click, be promoted into Ableton Live's own browser as a normalized .wav, and be handed to a mix agent that renders six taste variants for A/B feedback.</p>
      <p>None of that existed in the first version of this site. Three weeks ago this was a default-Squarespace-shaped portfolio that couldn't play a single audio file. What follows is a story about how the row in a catalog became a workbench, told through real git commits and live components you can click.</p>

      <CSPullquote hero>
        The page count went down as the card got more powerful. Five portal tabs collapsed into three. Each row now does the work the old tabs were supposed to.
      </CSPullquote>
    </div>

    <CSStatGrid
      stats={[
        { n: 20279, label: "rows indexed", color: "white" },
        { n: 14, label: "card verbs (was 2)", color: "yellow" },
        { n: 3, label: "portal tabs (was 5+)", color: "cyan" },
        { n: 6, label: "mix variants per job", color: "pink" },
      ]}
    />
  </CSSection>

  {/* ─────── 02 · THE CARD GREW UP — capability evolution ─────── */}
  <CSSection num="02" title="The card grew up" deck="Seven eras. Each one a real git commit where the row earned a new verb. Drag the slider. Watch the verbs light up underneath as the row learns what it can do.">
    <CSLiveDemo
      label="LIVE · DRAG TO SCRUB"
      caption="The card on the left morphs in place as you scrub. The chip strip below shows the verbs the card has earned. Note the page count assumptions in the right column shifting as the card carries more of the work."
      bleed
      pad="comfy"
    >
      <CardEvolutionScrubber />
    </CSLiveDemo>

    <div class="prose-block">
      <p>The pattern that emerged: every time the row could do more by itself, a whole UI surface could be deleted. The Listen page went away once the waveform played. The Library tab went away once Buckets could filter and sort. Workbench went away once cropping and stem-splitting moved into the card's expand panel. The portal got smaller and more powerful in the same week.</p>
    </div>
  </CSSection>

  {/* ─────── 03 · TRY THE CARD ─────── */}
  <CSSection num="03" title="Try the card" deck="Three live BucketCards in different states. Click the title to rename it. Click the waveform to play. Toggle categories. Hold ✕ to archive.">
    <CSLiveDemo
      label="LIVE · TRY EACH"
      caption="The actual production card markup with mock data. Three real states: a loop with all the expand-panel verbs visible, a voice memo, and a loop already promoted to Live."
      bleed
    >
      <BucketCardShowcase
        title="NOIZU_125_shaker_loop_tight_01"
        ext=".wav"
        duration="0:04"
        bpm={99}
        keyStr="E major"
        date={{ rel: "1y", stamp: "06-15-2024", days: "704d" }}
        bucket="loops"
        tags={["loop", "sample", "drum"]}
        contentType="music-only"
        sourceType="splice"
        expanded
      />
    </CSLiveDemo>

    <div class="card-grid-2">
      <BucketCardShowcase
        cardId="bc-voice"
        title="VOX_Tolo_0007"
        ext=".wav"
        duration="0:30"
        date={{ rel: "1y", stamp: "10-23-2024", days: "574d" }}
        silenceRatio={0.42}
        bucket="field"
        contentType="voice-note"
        sourceType="voice_memo"
        tags={["idea-fragment", "first-person"]}
      />
      <BucketCardShowcase
        cardId="bc-inlive"
        title="55_Cmaj_0_what_you_know"
        ext=".wav"
        duration="0:08"
        bpm={55}
        keyStr="C major"
        date={{ rel: "5d", stamp: "05-17-2026", days: "5d" }}
        bucket="loops"
        inLive
        contentType="loop"
        tags={["loop", "sample"]}
        expanded
      />
    </div>
  </CSSection>

  {/* ─────── 04 · THE PROMOTE PIPELINE ─────── */}
  <CSSection num="04" title="The card writes to Ableton" deck="One click. Three steps. The loop lands in Live's own browser, normalized, named by BPM and key, ready to drag onto a clip slot.">
    <div class="prose-block">
      <p>This is the move that made the catalog feel like part of the studio instead of a thing I had to switch to. Ableton's Browser already lets you drag audio onto a clip slot from any folder you've registered as a Place. So the engine writes into a Place I registered once. The DAW's own gesture (drag from sidebar into clip slot) does the work. No custom Max device, no plugin, no audio API. Just a file written to a folder Live already watches.</p>
    </div>

    <CSLiveDemo
      label="LIVE · CLICK PROMOTE"
      caption="The button is real. The pipeline is real (copy → loudnorm → set live_loop_path on the row). The animation timing approximates the actual sub-second-per-loop M1 behavior; loudnorm is the slowest of the three."
    >
      <PromoteButtonDemo />
    </CSLiveDemo>

    <div class="prose-block">
      <p>Earlier in the build I tried to put a Max-for-Live device inside Ableton instead. Two architectural walls killed it (the device's webview can't propagate drag events out, and Live's LiveAPI can't create audio clips programmatically). Once that direction was dead, the right move surfaced: stop building a synthetic surface inside the DAW, write into the surface the DAW already has. The Promote button is the entire bridge.</p>
    </div>
  </CSSection>

  {/* ─────── 05 · THE ENGINEER ─────── */}
  <CSSection num="05" title="The Engineer" deck="A per-track mix agent that learns my taste through A/B feedback. Three taste axes. Six variants per job. Free-text comments that carry forward to the next render.">
    <div class="prose-block">
      <p>The Engineer is the second pipeline downstream of the row. Where Promote-to-Live ships a clean loop into the DAW, the Engineer ships six full-mix takes back into the catalog for me to judge. Three taste axes:</p>
      <ul class="cs-axes">
        <li><strong class="ax-cyan">focal</strong> — forward_bright vs recessed_warm</li>
        <li><strong class="ax-pink">space</strong> — punchy_dry vs spacious_wet</li>
        <li><strong class="ax-yellow">dynamics</strong> — modern_compressed vs vintage_open</li>
      </ul>
      <p>The UI is one pair card. A on the left, B on the right. Click to switch playback sides. Free-text comment per pair persists into <code>mix_judgements</code>. The next render on the same source reads the full comment history into the planner's prompt. A space-axis retry mentioning a buried kick will also address a vocal complaint from an earlier focal pair on the same track. Cross-axis memory works; verified in production 2026-05-21.</p>
      <p>The planner is told the source defines the focal element, the masking conflicts, the stem relationships. Processing-for-the-sake-of-processing is the failure mode it's instructed to avoid. The renderer uses Pedalboard. After every variant render, five additional Pedalboard chains simulate phone / AirPods / laptop / car / club playback and translate per-band energy deltas into plain-language flags: "phone: vocal disappears", "club: stereo collapses", "translates clean".</p>
    </div>

    <CSLiveDemo
      label="LIVE · TINDER A vs B"
      caption="The production pair card. Click A/B (or ←/→), pick a side, leave a comment. Comments persist into mix_judgements and cross-pollinate the next render on the same source — the engineer learns my taste one pair at a time."
      bleed
      pad="comfy"
    >
      <EngineerPairCard />
    </CSLiveDemo>

    <div class="prose-block">
      <p>Phase 5 is in design: a reference catalog of artists I love (Tame Impala, Frank Ocean, 070 Shake, Travis Scott, Bon Iver) with per-track notes on what I like. The planner will pull closest references for THIS source into prompt context as taste anchors. v1 text-anchor only; v2 audio-derived feature similarity.</p>
    </div>
  </CSSection>

  {/* ─────── 06 · THE ENGINE SURFACE ─────── */}
  <CSSection num="06" title="The verbs underneath" deck="Every interaction in the card hits one of these endpoints. The catalog is the source of truth. The verbs are the contract. Surfaces are disposable.">
    <CSLiveDemo
      label="LIVE · CLICK A VERB"
      caption="When Surprise and Library got deleted on 2026-05-17, the catalog didn't change. Three surfaces collapsed into one. That's what verbs-as-contract buys: I can throw out the UI without throwing out the data."
      bleed
      pad="comfy"
    >
      <VerbsHub />
    </CSLiveDemo>

    <CSPullquote cite="from the session log that triggered the 3-tab consolidation, 2026-05-17">
      "i think at this point it'd make sense to consolidate the console's ui. library doesn't make sense anymore as a separate page, the bucket's page is really the library now... 'surprise' page also feels like bloat now."
    </CSPullquote>
  </CSSection>

  {/* ─────── 07 · THE BOUNDARY ─────── */}
  <CSSection num="07" title="What it deliberately does not do" deck="A music tool I trust is a music tool that does not pretend to author. This list is short on purpose and gets checked before any feature lands.">
    <ul class="wont">
      <li>Write Substack, LinkedIn, or Instagram posts without an explicit draft invocation.</li>
      <li>Propose albums, EPs, or song concepts.</li>
      <li>Push daily prompts.</li>
      <li>Listen to voice commands during a session.</li>
      <li>Touch the desktop.</li>
      <li>Publish to streaming platforms.</li>
      <li>Make creative decisions on my behalf.</li>
    </ul>
    <div class="prose-block">
      <p>Every one of those is a feature an adjacent product ships. Every one is a place where the machine would pretend to author. A catalog that emails me an unsolicited "draft album concept" stops being a catalog and becomes a parasocial collaborator. That trade was off the table from the start.</p>
      <p>Whisper transcribes. Claude classifies. The Engineer renders variants and reasons about them. None of them synthesize. The synthesis happens later, in Live, by hand.</p>
    </div>
  </CSSection>

  {/* ─────── 08 · CLOSING ─────── */}
  <CSSection num="08" title="Where this points" deck="The point of the build is not the build. The point is more music made.">
    <div class="prose-block">
      <p><code>uxjon.com/sounds</code> already exists as a public route. Items flip to public via a toggle on any card. The infrastructure for the future state (a public-facing surface of finished work that streams from the same engine that catalogs the raw material) is already shipped. The path from "this loop is interesting" to "this track is out" runs through the same database and the same verbs.</p>
      <p>3,750 rows still sit in the inbox bucket. 215 promotable loops are queued. The machine just made choosing cheaper.</p>
    </div>

    <CSPullquote>
      Utility, not synthesis. The computer sorts, fingerprints, surfaces, and removes format friction. The artist still chooses, still drags, still arranges.
    </CSPullquote>
  </CSSection>

  <div class="cs-foot">
    <a href="/#work" class="cs-foot-back">← back to selected work</a>
  </div>
</CSLayout>

<style>
  .prose-block { max-width: 64ch; }
  .prose-block > .cs-pullquote { max-width: 64ch; }

  .card-grid-2 {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr;
    gap: 18px;
    margin: 24px 0 8px;
  }
  @media (max-width: 880px) {
    .card-grid-2 { grid-template-columns: 1fr; }
  }

  .cs-axes {
    list-style: none;
    padding: 0;
    margin: 18px 0 22px;
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(280px, 1fr));
    gap: 8px 22px;
    max-width: 64ch;
  }
  .cs-axes li {
    font-family: ui-monospace, SFMono-Regular, Menlo, monospace;
    font-size: 13px;
    color: rgba(245, 241, 232, 0.85);
    padding: 8px 12px;
    border: 1px solid rgba(245, 241, 232, 0.10);
    border-radius: 3px;
    background: rgba(245, 241, 232, 0.02);
  }
  .cs-axes .ax-cyan { color: var(--brand-cyan); font-style: italic; }
  .cs-axes .ax-pink { color: var(--brand-pink); font-style: italic; }
  .cs-axes .ax-yellow { color: var(--brand-yellow); font-style: italic; }

  .wont {
    list-style: none;
    padding: 0;
    margin: 18px 0 28px;
    max-width: 64ch;
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr;
    gap: 6px 22px;
  }
  @media (max-width: 720px) {
    .wont { grid-template-columns: 1fr; }
  }
  .wont li {
    position: relative;
    padding-left: 22px;
    font-family: ui-monospace, SFMono-Regular, Menlo, monospace;
    font-size: 13px;
    color: rgba(245, 241, 232, 0.78);
    line-height: 1.5;
  }
  .wont li::before {
    content: "—";
    position: absolute;
    left: 0;
    color: var(--brand-pink);
  }

  .cs-foot {
    text-align: center;
    padding: 64px 0 0;
  }
  .cs-foot-back {
    font-family: ui-monospace, SFMono-Regular, Menlo, monospace;
    font-size: 11px;
    letter-spacing: 0.22em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--brand-orange);
    text-decoration: none;
    padding: 8px 16px;
    border: 1px solid rgba(255, 90, 60, 0.32);
    border-radius: 2px;
    transition: color 160ms ease, border-color 160ms ease, background 160ms ease;
  }
  .cs-foot-back:hover {
    color: #f5f1e8;
    border-color: var(--brand-orange);
    background: rgba(255, 90, 60, 0.08);
  }
</style>
